WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.775
Rotation succeeds only when consumers
change their behavior.

2
00:00:03.775 --> 00:00:07.629
Updating a database password in a secret
store does not

3
00:00:07.629 --> 00:00:11.674
update a long-running Node.js process that
cached the old value.

4
00:00:11.674 --> 00:00:14.950
It may not terminate existing database
sessions either.

5
00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:18.803
Define how each client reacquires
credentials and how connection pools

6
00:00:18.803 --> 00:00:22.464
replace or drain connections when required
by the service.

7
00:00:22.464 --> 00:00:25.257
For short-lived tokens, use
provider-supported refresh before

8
00:00:25.257 --> 00:00:28.147
expiration with the platform's recommended
safety margin.

9
00:00:28.147 --> 00:00:29.496
Keep clocks synchronized.

10
00:00:29.496 --> 00:00:31.808
Coordinate refreshes so concurrent
requests do

11
00:00:31.808 --> 00:00:33.927
not overwhelm the identity service.

12
00:00:33.927 --> 00:00:37.106
A rejected token may justify one
controlled refresh,

13
00:00:37.106 --> 00:00:40.767
but permission denial should not trigger
endless renewal attempts.

14
00:00:40.767 --> 00:00:43.271
Distinguish authentication failure,
authorization failure, and

15
00:00:43.271 --> 00:00:45.776
temporary network failure in sanitized
diagnostics.

16
00:00:45.776 --> 00:00:49.437
For reusable credentials, some services
support an overlap sequence:

17
00:00:49.437 --> 00:00:52.712
activate a new credential, temporarily
accept both versions,

18
00:00:52.712 --> 00:00:56.565
migrate consumers, verify adoption, and
revoke the old one.

19
00:00:56.565 --> 00:00:59.648
Bound that overlap and track which
consumers still

20
00:00:59.648 --> 00:01:02.538
use the old version through safe metadata.

21
00:01:02.538 --> 00:01:05.620
Other services support only one active
credential or

22
00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:09.185
manage short-lived credentials themselves,
so the exact rollout and

23
00:01:09.185 --> 00:01:12.460
downtime constraints must be established
with that service.

24
00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:16.506
Caching needs an explicit maximum age and
a refresh strategy.

25
00:01:16.506 --> 00:01:18.914
Rotation tests must cover running
processes,

26
00:01:18.914 --> 00:01:21.130
new instances, and pooled connections.

27
00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:25.079
Confirm what revocation actually
invalidates; changing a stored password is

28
00:01:25.079 --> 00:01:29.125
different from ending established sessions
or revoking an issued token.

29
00:01:29.125 --> 00:01:31.437
An emergency compromise response may
require

30
00:01:31.437 --> 00:01:33.556
faster cutoff than routine rotation.

31
00:01:33.556 --> 00:01:37.891
When an identity provider or secret store
is unavailable, use bounded

32
00:01:37.891 --> 00:01:42.419
retries with backoff and jitter, then
report a clear dependency failure.

33
00:01:42.419 --> 00:01:45.502
Continue with previously acquired
credentials only while they

34
00:01:45.502 --> 00:01:48.392
remain valid and policy permits their use.

35
00:01:48.392 --> 00:01:50.318
Never substitute a hard-coded emergency

36
00:01:50.318 --> 00:01:52.437
credential or silently broaden
permissions.

37
00:01:52.437 --> 00:01:55.231
Preserve availability through deliberate
resilience design, with

38
00:01:55.231 --> 00:01:58.410
deadlines and operational visibility,
rather than through a

39
00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:01.300
permanent fallback that defeats the
identity architecture.

